      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Hugh Forbes founded the town in 1792, laying out Locust Street and selling lots that would become the city’s first permanent settlement.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mtsterlingchamber.com/about/history",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1796, Mount Sterling became the county seat of newly created Montgomery County, establishing a courthouse that would be the first of seven in town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Sterling%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town’s early economy thrived around a large brick market house where farmers sold produce, cementing Mount Sterling as the region’s commercial hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Sterling%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "A 125‑foot tree‑covered mound, later proven a burial site, now sits at the intersection of Queen and Locust streets in downtown.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Sterling%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Mount Sterling hosts the Gateway Regional Arts Center, housed in a historic First United Methodist Church listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
